The Deferrable Server is the most directly relevant predecessor. It improves
aperiodic response time by preserving unused budget within a server period,
but this preservation can cause back-to-back execution of up to $2Q_s$ in a
short interval, effectively violating the schedulability assumptions of the
periodic task set~\cite{buttazzo}. The CBS eliminates this problem through
its deadline assignment rules.

The Total Bandwidth Server (TBS) operates under EDF and assigns each
arriving aperiodic job $k$ the deadline
$d_k = \max(r_k, d_{k-1}) + C_k / U_s$~\cite{buttazzo}.
TBS is simpler than CBS but uses the \emph{actual} computation time $C_k$,
which is only known at job completion. This means TBS cannot provide
isolation against overrunning tasks --- a job that executes longer than
expected pushes all subsequent deadlines further out. The CBS resolves
this through an explicit budget variable~\cite{abeni1998}.

% ============================================================
\section{CBS Definition}
% ============================================================

Following Buttazzo~\cite{buttazzo}, pp.~189--190, a CBS is characterized by:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $Q_s$: the maximum server budget (execution units per period)
  \item $T_s$: the server period
  \item $U_s = Q_s / T_s$: the server bandwidth
  \item $c_s$: the current remaining budget, $0 < c_s \leq Q_s$
  \item $d_{s,k}$: the current server deadline, initialized as $d_{s,0} = 0$
\end{itemize}

Each aperiodic job served by the CBS is assigned the current server deadline
$d_{s,k}$ as its scheduling deadline and is inserted into the EDF ready queue.
Whenever a job executes, $c_s$ decreases by the same amount. When $c_s = 0$,
the budget is immediately recharged to $Q_s$ and a new server deadline is
generated as $d_{s,k+1} = d_{s,k} + T_s$.

The CBS is called \emph{active} at time $t$ if pending jobs exist (since
$c_s > 0$ always holds), and \emph{idle} otherwise.

% ============================================================
\section{CBS Algorithm}
% ============================================================

The CBS behavior is governed by three rules, formalized in
Buttazzo~\cite{buttazzo}, p.~192 (Figure~6.15).

\textbf{Rule 1 --- New job arrival when server is idle,
$r_j + (c_s / Q_s) \cdot T_s \geq d_{s,k}$:}
A new server period is started. Set $d_{s,k+1} = r_j + T_s$ and
$c_s \leftarrow Q_s$. Assign deadline $d_{s,k+1}$ to the job.

\textbf{Rule 2 --- New job arrival when server is idle,
$r_j + (c_s / Q_s) \cdot T_s < d_{s,k}$:}
The current budget is still fresh. Assign the existing deadline $d_{s,k}$
to the job without replenishment. The budget $c_s$ remains unchanged.

\textbf{Rule 3 --- Budget exhaustion ($c_s = 0$):}
Generate a new deadline $d_{s,k+1} = d_{s,k} + T_s$ and replenish
$c_s \leftarrow Q_s$. The running job continues with the new (later)
deadline, effectively reducing its EDF priority.

When the server is active (already serving a job), arriving jobs are
enqueued in FIFO order. When a job finishes, the next pending job is
served using the current budget and deadline.

The condition in Rule~1 vs. Rule~2 determines whether the remaining
budget is ``fresh enough'' to serve the new job within the current server
period without causing the demand to exceed $U_s$. This condition is the
key mechanism that distinguishes CBS from TBS and prevents the
overload interference that affects DS.

% ============================================================
\section{Scheduling Example}
% ============================================================

Following the example in Buttazzo~\cite{buttazzo}, pp.~190--191
(Figure~6.14), consider a hard periodic task $\tau_1$ with $C_1 = 4$,
$T_1 = 7$, and a soft task $\tau_2$ served by a CBS with $Q_s = 3$,
$T_s = 8$ (so $U_s = 3/8$). The schedulability condition gives:
$4/7 + 3/8 = 0.946 \leq 1$ --- the system is feasible.

The first job of $\tau_2$ arrives at $r_1 = 3$ when the server is idle.
Since $c_s \geq (d_0 - r_1) \cdot U_s$, Rule~1 applies: a new deadline
$d_1 = r_1 + T_s = 11$ is set and $c_s = 3$.
At $t = 7$, the budget is exhausted (Rule~3): $d_2 = 11 + 8 = 19$ and
$c_s = 3$. Since $d_2 = 19 > d_{\tau_1} = 14$, $\tau_1$ preempts and
executes. $\tau_2$ resumes at $t = 11$ and finishes at $t = 12$ with
$c_s = 2$.

The second job arrives at $r_2 = 13$. Since $c_s = 2 < (d_2 - r_2) \cdot U_s$,
Rule~2 applies: the existing deadline $d_2 = 19$ is reused. At $t = 15$,
budget exhaustion again triggers Rule~3: $d_3 = 27$, $c_s = 3$. $\tau_1$
preempts until $t = 19$, after which $\tau_2$ finishes the second job
at $t = 20$.

Throughout this example, $\tau_1$ meets all its deadlines despite $\tau_2$
executing more than $Q_s$ in total --- confirming the isolation property.

% ============================================================
\section{CBS Properties}
% ============================================================

\subsection{Isolation Theorem}

The central property of CBS is formally stated in
Buttazzo~\cite{buttazzo}, p.~192, as:

\textbf{Theorem~6.8}~\cite{buttazzo}: \textit{The CPU utilization of a CBS
with parameters $(Q_s, T_s)$ is $U_s = Q_s / T_s$, independently of the
computation times and arrival pattern of the served jobs.}

This theorem means the CBS behaves like a periodic task with utilization
$U_s$ in EDF analysis, regardless of how much work aperiodic tasks actually
demand. The proof is given in Abeni and Buttazzo~\cite{abeni1998}.

\subsection{Feasibility Lemma}

\textbf{Lemma~6.6}~\cite{buttazzo}: \textit{Given $n$ periodic hard tasks
with total utilization $U_p$ and $m$ CBS servers with total utilization
$U_s = \sum_{i=1}^{m} U_{s_i}$, the whole set is schedulable by EDF if
and only if:}
\begin{equation}
  U_p + U_s \leq 1
  \label{eq:feasibility}
\end{equation}

This result allows CBS to be integrated into standard EDF schedulability
analysis without any additional complexity --- a significant advantage over DS.

\subsection{EDF Equivalence Lemma}

\textbf{Lemma~6.7}~\cite{buttazzo}: \textit{A hard task $\tau_i$ with
parameters $(C_i, T_i)$ is schedulable by a CBS with $Q_s \geq C_i$ and
$T_s = T_i$ if and only if $\tau_i$ is schedulable with EDF.}

This lemma shows that CBS degenerates to a plain EDF algorithm when
serving tasks whose parameters match the server parameters exactly.

\subsection{Worst-Case Response Time}

Buttazzo~\cite{buttazzo}, pp.~196--198, derives the worst-case response
time for a job with computation time $C_i$ served by a CBS:
\begin{equation}
  R_i = C_i + \left\lceil \frac{C_i}{Q_s} \right\rceil (T_s - Q_s)
  \label{eq:responsetime}
\end{equation}
This answers the open question identified in Milestone~1. The minimum
worst-case response time $C_i / U_s$ is achieved when $C_i$ is a perfect
multiple of $Q_s$. When context-switch overhead $\epsilon$ is included,
the response time becomes:
\begin{equation}
  R_i = C_i + \left\lceil \frac{C_i}{T_s U_s - \epsilon} \right\rceil
  (T_s - T_s U_s + \epsilon)
  \label{eq:responseoverhead}
\end{equation}

The optimal $T_s$ minimizing average response time is given by
Buttazzo~\cite{buttazzo}, p.~201, Eq.~(6.15):
\begin{equation}
  T_s = \frac{1}{U_s} \left( \epsilon + \sqrt{\frac{\epsilon C^{\mathrm{avg}}}{1 - U_s}} \right)
  \label{eq:optimalTs}
\end{equation}

