\section{Critical Evaluation}
% ============================================================

\subsection{Strengths}

CBS provides several concrete technical advantages:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Formal isolation guarantee:} Theorem~6.8 ensures that
        aperiodic overruns cannot affect periodic task schedulability,
        unlike DS whose budget preservation can violate the RM bound.
  \item \textbf{Work-conserving:} CBS exploits available slack immediately
        when a job arrives and the budget is fresh (Rule~2), providing
        better aperiodic response time than non-work-conserving approaches
        such as PS.
  \item \textbf{Simple schedulability test:} Eq.~(\ref{eq:feasibility})
        requires no separate analysis beyond the standard EDF test.
  \item \textbf{Closed-form response time bound:} Eq.~(\ref{eq:responsetime})
        provides a tractable worst-case analysis.
  \item \textbf{Low runtime overhead:} $O(1)$ server state update per
        scheduling event; constant memory per server instance.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations and Hidden Assumptions}

CBS has several significant practical constraints:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{EDF dependency:} CBS requires an EDF-capable scheduler.
        Many industrial RTOS (AUTOSAR OS, classic FreeRTOS) use
        fixed-priority scheduling and cannot run CBS without modification.
        The Sporadic Server is the appropriate fixed-priority analog.
  \item \textbf{No hard per-job guarantee:} CBS bounds bandwidth, not
        individual aperiodic job deadlines. Hard aperiodic tasks require
        different mechanisms.
  \item \textbf{Static parameter selection:} $Q_s$ and $T_s$ are chosen
        offline. Under variable aperiodic load, a fixed pair may be
        either wasteful or insufficiently isolating.
  \item \textbf{Single-processor formulation:} The original CBS does not
        extend directly to multi-core platforms~\cite{buttazzo}.
  \item \textbf{Implicit deadline assumption:} Lemma~6.6 assumes
        $D_i = T_i$ for periodic tasks. Constrained deadlines require
        further analysis.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Comparison with TBS}

Simulation results in Buttazzo~\cite{buttazzo}, pp.~194--196
(Figures~6.16--6.18), show that CBS and TBS achieve comparable mean
tardiness under moderate soft load. However, CBS outperforms TBS when
task computation times have high variance
(Figure~6.18 in~\cite{buttazzo}), because TBS relies on exact WCET
knowledge at job arrival, which may be unavailable or unreliable.
The CBS uses an explicit budget to decouple server behavior from
actual execution times, which is the key engineering advantage over TBS
in systems with unpredictable aperiodic workloads.

% ============================================================
\section{Application Analysis}
% ============================================================

\textbf{Suitable scenario --- Robotic arm controller:}
A system with periodic joint control at 1~kHz (hard RT, $U_p = 0.6$)
and soft event-driven network handlers. A CBS with $U_s = 0.3$ reserves
30\% bandwidth for handlers. By Lemma~6.6, $0.6 + 0.3 = 0.9 \leq 1$
--- schedulable. Network overruns cannot affect the control loop.
Linux \texttt{SCHED\_DEADLINE} implements a CBS-like mechanism
suitable for this scenario.

\textbf{Suitable scenario --- Multimedia streaming:}
Buttazzo~\cite{buttazzo} identifies continuous media applications as the
primary motivation for CBS. Variable-length codec outputs create
unpredictable execution times --- exactly the condition where CBS
outperforms TBS (Figure~6.18 in~\cite{buttazzo}).

\textbf{Unsuitable scenario --- AUTOSAR automotive ECU:}
AUTOSAR OS mandates fixed-priority scheduling (OSEK standard). CBS
cannot be deployed without replacing the scheduler. The Sporadic Server
is the correct mechanism in this context.

\textbf{Unsuitable scenario --- Hard aperiodic tasks:}
CBS provides bandwidth guarantees, not per-job deadline guarantees.
Safety-critical interrupt handlers requiring certified response time bounds
cannot rely on CBS alone.

% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}
% ============================================================

The Constant Bandwidth Server is a formally rigorous and practically
useful mechanism for integrating aperiodic task service into EDF-scheduled
real-time systems. Its isolation property (Theorem~6.8) and simple
feasibility test (Lemma~6.6) make it analytically superior to its
predecessors, particularly the Deferrable Server. The closed-form
worst-case response time (Eq.~\ref{eq:responsetime}) and optimal
parameter selection formula (Eq.~\ref{eq:optimalTs}) provide tractable
design tools.

The primary limitation is the requirement for an EDF runtime, which
restricts deployment on fixed-priority RTOS. For systems that can support
EDF, CBS provides a principled solution to the coexistence of hard periodic
and soft aperiodic workloads, with performance comparable to TBS under
normal conditions and superior isolation under overload.

Open directions include multi-core CBS extensions, adaptive parameter
tuning, and integration with resource-sharing protocols such as SRP.

